wifi_db: clamp channelWidth to 20MHz + set lab country under HWSIM_RADIO (single-phy tri-band)

init_radio_config_default() defaults the 5GHz radio to 80MHz and the 6GHz radio to
160MHz. On FEATURE_SINGLE_PHY (one mac80211_hwsim wiphy projected as wifi0/wifi1/wifi2),
Linux 7.0 brings up two concurrent AP channel contexts on one phy at 20MHz, but rejects
a 2nd/3rd context at 80/160MHz (-EINVAL when START_AP sets the channel). Proven with a
clean host-hwsim test: tst0@2437 + tst1@5180 both reach AP-ENABLED at 20MHz, but the
80MHz VIF does not. So the wide defaults make wifi1 (5GHz/80) and wifi2 (6GHz/160) fail
to beacon while wifi0 (2.4GHz/20) is up.

Clamp channelWidth to 20MHz under HWSIM_RADIO so the concurrent contexts fit and tri-band
comes up. For 6GHz that is not sufficient by itself: the operating class encodes the
width (131=20, 132=40, 133=80, 134=160), and the default 134 (160MHz) left with a 20MHz
width is an inconsistent chandef that makes the 6GHz START_AP -EINVAL. Also set the 6GHz
operating class to 131 (20MHz) so width and op-class agree. (5GHz came up with its
class-128 default because that band's chandef derives the width from channelWidth.)
Pairs with the rdk-wifi-hal FEATURE_SINGLE_PHY fix (0022) that drops the pre-START_AP
standalone SET_WIPHY channel-set. Real BananaPi R4 hardware keeps its wide-channel
defaults (this is hwsim-only).

Third: set the default country to a 6GHz-IR lab domain (GB) under HWSIM_RADIO. The code
here first tries wifi_hal_get_default_country_code(); in this hwsim build the platform
default-country hook is absent, so it returns error and the country stays the hardcoded
fallback wifi_countrycode_US. US 6GHz is NO-IR (PASSIVE) in wireless-regdb, so OneWifi
drives the effective cfg80211 core reg to US and wifi2 (6GHz, ch37/6135) fails START_AP
with -EINVAL (cfg80211_reg_check_beaconing on a NO-IR channel). GB 6GHz is IR-capable
(LPI, 23 dBm) so wifi2 can beacon. A standalone-hostapd A/B/C/D matrix proved 5975 and
6135 both reach AP-ENABLED under GB, alone and as a 3rd concurrent AP on one phy. Real
BananaPi R4 hardware supplies its own platform country and keeps US/its default.

---
 source/db/wifi_db.c | 25 +++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+)

--- a/source/db/wifi_db.c
+++ b/source/db/wifi_db.c
@@ -189,6 +189,23 @@
             break;
     }

+#if defined(HWSIM_RADIO)
+    /* FEATURE_SINGLE_PHY on mac80211_hwsim: all logical radios share ONE physical
+     * wiphy. On Linux 7.0 two concurrent AP channel contexts come up on one phy at
+     * 20 MHz, but a 2nd/3rd context at 80/160 MHz is rejected (-EINVAL at START_AP)
+     * -- proven with a host-hwsim test (two 20 MHz APs up concurrently, 80 MHz not).
+     * The default 80 MHz (5 GHz) / 160 MHz (6 GHz) therefore make wifi1/wifi2 fail to
+     * beacon. Clamp to 20 MHz so tri-band comes up. Pairs with the rdk-wifi-hal
+     * single-phy fix that drops the pre-START_AP standalone SET_WIPHY channel-set.
+     * Real BananaPi R4 hardware keeps its wide-channel defaults. */
+    cfg.channelWidth = WIFI_CHANNELBANDWIDTH_20MHZ;
+    /* 6 GHz operating class encodes the width (131=20, 132=40, 133=80, 134=160). The
+     * default is 134; with the width clamped to 20 MHz that tuple is inconsistent and
+     * hostap's 6 GHz chandef makes START_AP -EINVAL. Use class 131 to match 20 MHz. */
+    if (cfg.band == WIFI_FREQUENCY_6_BAND)
+        cfg.operatingClass = 131;
+#endif /* HWSIM_RADIO */
+
     for (int i=0; i<radio_capab.channel_list[0].num_channels; i++)
     {
         cfg.channel_map[i].ch_number = radio_capab.channel_list[0].channels_list[i];
@@ -232,6 +249,14 @@
         }
     }
     cfg.countryCode = country_code_val;
+#if defined(HWSIM_RADIO)
+    /* The platform default-country hook is absent in this hwsim build, so the code
+     * above falls back to wifi_countrycode_US. US 6 GHz is NO-IR (PASSIVE) in
+     * wireless-regdb, so wifi2 (6 GHz) fails START_AP with -EINVAL
+     * (cfg80211_reg_check_beaconing). Override to a 6 GHz-IR lab domain so the single
+     * hwsim phy can beacon 6 GHz. Real hardware keeps its platform/US default. */
+    cfg.countryCode = wifi_countrycode_GB;
+#endif /* HWSIM_RADIO */
     cfg.operatingEnvironment = wifi_operating_env_indoor;
     cfg.dtimPeriod = 1;
     if (cfg.beaconInterval == 0) {
